    "title": "UConn men vs. UCLA in NCAA Tournament: Start time, TV",
    "text": "PHILADELPHIA, PENNSYLVANIA - MARCH 20: Alex Karaban #11 of the UConn Huskies walks off the court after win against the Furman Paladins during the first round of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament held at Xfinity Mobile Arena.Ben Solomon/NCAA Photos via Getty ImagesTarris Reed Jr. guided UConn men's basketball past a surprising upset bid from Furman Friday evening, setting up a second-round matchup with No. 7 seed UCLA. The Huskies and the Bruins will tip off at 8:45 p.m. Sunday evening, set for a slightly earlier start than Friday's delayed 10:30 p.m. opening tip. TNT will broadcast the action, and the three-man broadcast crew of Andrew Catalon, Steve Lappas and Evan Washburn will return in the booth for Sunday's matchup. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢UConn opens as an early 4.5-point favorite, according to DraftKings, a line that could be subject to significant fluctuations as both teams await key injury updates. Starting Huskies point guard Silas Demary Jr. missed his first career college basketball game due to injury Friday, alongside reserve wing Jaylin Stewart, while UCLA's leading scorer Tyler Bilodeau missed the Bruins' tournament opener. More For YouDespite the absence of Bilodeau, UCLA embarked on a dominant 18-3 run early in the first half and enjoyed a double-digit lead over No. 7 UCF throughout much of Friday's first matchup from Philadelphia. UCF ultimately whittled down its deficit to just a single possession during the final seconds, but Skyy Clark's split pair of free throws was enough to secure a 75-71 win and passage to the Round of 32. Sunday's matchup will once again be held at the Philadelphia 76ers' Xfinity Mobile Arena, where the Furman contingent often outcheered the traveling UConn fans thanks to a glob of Paladin-leaning neutral supporters. With a win, the Huskies will head down further south along Interstate-95 to Washington, D.C., where they would face the winner of Michigan State and Louisville in the Sweet 16. Article continues below this ad CT Insider LogoGet daily updates from the UConn basketball world.",
